export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"mepdraft","name":"MEP drafting","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"mepdraft-start","scope":"addon:mepdraft","title":"MEP drafting: getting started","summary":"Draw pipes and ducts on service layers, drop valves and equipment symbols that cut into the line, tag them, and count them, all as ordinary geometry and blocks.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["mep","hvac","plumbing","heating","pipe","duct","valve","symbol","radiator","air handling unit","diffuser","damper","service","layer","tag","legend","schedule","count","single line","double line"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"switch-it-on","text":"Switch it on","level":2},{"id":"set-the-scale-first","text":"Set the scale first","level":2},{"id":"services","text":"Services","level":2},{"id":"a-small-heating-plan-step-by-step","text":"A small heating plan, step by step","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMEP drafting is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It is for 2D building-services drawings: heating, chilled water, domestic water, drainage, gas, fire and ventilation. You draw runs on service layers, put valves and accessories on them, place equipment, tag everything and count it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything it draws is ordinary: runs are lines, arcs and polylines on the service&#039;s layer, symbols and equipment are blocks with attributes. You can move, copy, trim and stretch them with the usual tools, and a DXF or DWG file carries them as plain geometry and blocks on the right layers. Someone who opens the file without the add-on sees and edits the same drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"switch-it-on\"\u003ESwitch it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you add it: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMEP drafting\u003C/span\u003E. Its commands then work at the command line, and its panel section lists every command, symbol and equipment as a button. A toolbar group can be switched on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMEP drafting\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-scale-first\"\u003ESet the scale first\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and give the scale the drawing will be printed at, for example \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E for 1:50. Symbols on single-line runs and all tag text are sized for that scale: a symbol is 6 mm long and text 2.5 mm high on paper (both can be changed in Settings).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"services\"\u003EServices\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA service is a layer with a colour, a linetype and a short tag. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSERVICE\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them: heating flow and return (HF, HR), chilled water flow and return (CHF, CHR), domestic cold, hot and circulation (DCW, DHW, DHC), waste and soil (W, S), gas (G), fire (F), refrigerant (REF), condensate (CD), supply, return and extract air (SA, RA, EA). Type a tag to draw in that service. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E adds your own service: its tag, name, layer, colour number and linetype. The layer is made the first time you draw in the service.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layer names, colours and linetypes are a starting set, not a layer standard. Rename the layers or add services to match your office.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-small-heating-plan-step-by-step\"\u003EA small heating plan, step by step\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPEQUIP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eradiator\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E1400\u003C/code\u003E, and click where the radiator goes. It gets the tag RAD-01.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPPIPE\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EService\u003C/span\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EHF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EDN20\u003C/code\u003E, then click the points of the flow run. Start or end near a radiator&#039;s connection stub and the run snaps onto it. Ortho and polar tracking work as in any drawing command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPPIPE\u003C/code\u003E again with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EService\u003C/span\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EHR\u003C/code\u003E for the return: it comes out dashed on its own layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSYM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eball\u003C/code\u003E, and click on the flow run: the line is broken and the valve sits in the gap, turned to the line, tagged V-01.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSIZETAG\u003C/code\u003E and click a run to write its size and service along it, for example \u003Ccode\u003EDN20 HF\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPFLOW\u003C/code\u003E puts a flow arrow on a run.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSCHED\u003C/code\u003E places the count as a table; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPLEGEND\u003C/code\u003E places a legend of what the drawing uses.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-runs\" data-topic=\"addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-runs\"\u003ERuns: pipes and ducts\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-symbols\" data-topic=\"addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-symbols\"\u003ESymbols, equipment and tags\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-lists\" data-topic=\"addon:mepdraft/mepdraft-lists\"\u003ELegend, schedule and what is not built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What it is MEP drafting is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It is for 2D building-services drawings: heating, chilled water, domestic water, drainage, gas, fire and ventilation. You draw runs on service layers, put valves and accessories on them, place equipment, tag everything and count it. Everything it draws is ordinary: runs are lines, arcs and polylines on the service's layer, symbols and equipment are blocks with attributes. You can move, copy, trim and stretch them with the usual tools, and a DXF or DWG file carries them as plain geometry and blocks on the right layers. Someone who opens the file without the add-on sees and edits the same drawing. Switch it on It is off until you add it: Settings › General › Add-ons › MEP drafting. Its commands then work at the command line, and its panel section lists every command, symbol and equipment as a button. A toolbar group can be switched on in Settings › CadCraft › MEP drafting. Set the scale first Type MEPSCALE and give the scale the drawing will be printed at, for example 50 for 1:50. Symbols on single-line runs and all tag text are sized for that scale: a symbol is 6 mm long and text 2.5 mm high on paper (both can be changed in Settings). Services A service is a layer with a colour, a linetype and a short tag. MEPSERVICE then ? lists them: heating flow and return (HF, HR), chilled water flow and return (CHF, CHR), domestic cold, hot and circulation (DCW, DHW, DHC), waste and soil (W, S), gas (G), fire (F), refrigerant (REF), condensate (CD), supply, return and extract air (SA, RA, EA). Type a tag to draw in that service. A adds your own service: its tag, name, layer, colour number and linetype. The layer is made the first time you draw in the service. The layer names, colours and linetypes are a starting set, not a layer standard. Rename the layers or add services to match your office. A small heating plan, step by step MEPSCALE, 50. MEPEQUIP, radiator, then Size and 1400, and click where the radiator goes. It gets the tag RAD-01. MEPPIPE: Service HF, Size DN20, then click the points of the flow run. Start or end near a radiator's connection stub and the run snaps onto it. Ortho and polar tracking work as in any drawing command. MEPPIPE again with Service HR for the return: it comes out dashed on its own layer. MEPSYM, ball, and click on the flow run: the line is broken and the valve sits in the gap, turned to the line, tagged V-01. MEPSIZETAG and click a run to write its size and service along it, for example DN20 HF. MEPFLOW puts a flow arrow on a run. MEPSCHED places the count as a table; MEPLEGEND places a legend of what the drawing uses. Where to go next Runs: pipes and ducts Symbols, equipment and tags Legend, schedule and what is not built yet"},{"slug":"mepdraft-runs","scope":"addon:mepdraft","title":"MEP drafting: pipe and duct runs","summary":"Single-line and double-line pipes by nominal size, rectangular, round and flexible ducts, branches, and how a run stays editable.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["pipe","duct","run","single line","double line","nominal size","dn","outside diameter","elbow","mitre","radius","flexible duct","branch","tee","service layer"],"headings":[{"id":"pipes","text":"Pipes","level":2},{"id":"ducts","text":"Ducts","level":2},{"id":"branches-and-connections","text":"Branches and connections","level":2},{"id":"editing-a-run","text":"Editing a run","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"pipes\"\u003EPipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPPIPE\u003C/code\u003E draws a pipe run through the points you click. Before the first point you can change:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EService\u003C/span\u003E: the service&#039;s tag, for example \u003Ccode\u003EHF\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E: a nominal size, \u003Ccode\u003EDN15\u003C/code\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003EDN600\u003C/code\u003E (in a drawing in inches: \u003Ccode\u003E1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3/4\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1-1/4\u003C/code\u003E and so on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELines\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E for a single line on the centreline, or \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E for a double line with the two edges at the pipe&#039;s real outside diameter and drawn elbows at the bends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe outside diameters are those of steel pipe (ASME B36.10M, the same table the Piping add-on uses). For copper, plastic or multilayer pipe type the outside diameter itself, for example \u003Ccode\u003EØ22\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E22mm\u003C/code\u003E; the run is drawn at exactly that and its size reads \u003Ccode\u003EØ22\u003C/code\u003E. There is no table of ours for those pipes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA single-line run is one polyline. A double-line run is a set of lines and arcs: two edges for each straight piece, two arcs for each elbow with a line across where the elbow starts and ends, and a line closing each open end. Elbows are drawn at a centreline radius of 1.5 times the outside diameter, a drawing choice.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ducts\"\u003EDucts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPDUCT\u003C/code\u003E draws a duct run. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E chooses:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E rectangular: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E is width by height, for example \u003Ccode\u003E400x250\u003C/code\u003E. The plan shows the width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E round: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E is the diameter, for example \u003Ccode\u003E200\u003C/code\u003E. It is drawn as a double line and its size reads \u003Ccode\u003EØ200\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E flexible: one wavy line as wide as the duct.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElbow\u003C/span\u003E chooses \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E radius elbows (centreline radius equal to the duct&#039;s width) or \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E mitred corners with the mitre line drawn.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"branches-and-connections\"\u003EBranches and connections\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStart or end a run on another run and it joins it. On a double-line run the branch stops at the main&#039;s edge and that edge is opened for it. Start or end a run near an equipment connection point and it snaps onto the point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing-a-run\"\u003EEditing a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run is plain geometry, so move, copy, stretch, trim, extend and erase work on it as on any line. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSELECT\u003C/code\u003E selects every piece of the run you click, with the symbols on it. Each piece remembers its service and size, not its position, so the size tag, the symbols and the legend keep working after you edit it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging a run&#039;s size or service after it is drawn is not built yet: erase it and draw it again, or move its pieces to another layer by hand (the size tag and schedule will still say the old service).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Pipes MEPPIPE draws a pipe run through the points you click. Before the first point you can change: Service: the service's tag, for example HF. Size: a nominal size, DN15 to DN600 (in a drawing in inches: 1/2, 3/4, 1, 1-1/4 and so on). Lines: S for a single line on the centreline, or D for a double line with the two edges at the pipe's real outside diameter and drawn elbows at the bends. The outside diameters are those of steel pipe (ASME B36.10M, the same table the Piping add-on uses). For copper, plastic or multilayer pipe type the outside diameter itself, for example Ø22 or 22mm; the run is drawn at exactly that and its size reads Ø22. There is no table of ours for those pipes. A single-line run is one polyline. A double-line run is a set of lines and arcs: two edges for each straight piece, two arcs for each elbow with a line across where the elbow starts and ends, and a line closing each open end. Elbows are drawn at a centreline radius of 1.5 times the outside diameter, a drawing choice. Ducts MEPDUCT draws a duct run. Kind chooses: R rectangular: Size is width by height, for example 400x250. The plan shows the width. O round: Size is the diameter, for example 200. It is drawn as a double line and its size reads Ø200. F flexible: one wavy line as wide as the duct. Elbow chooses R radius elbows (centreline radius equal to the duct's width) or M mitred corners with the mitre line drawn. Branches and connections Start or end a run on another run and it joins it. On a double-line run the branch stops at the main's edge and that edge is opened for it. Start or end a run near an equipment connection point and it snaps onto the point. Editing a run A run is plain geometry, so move, copy, stretch, trim, extend and erase work on it as on any line. MEPSELECT selects every piece of the run you click, with the symbols on it. Each piece remembers its service and size, not its position, so the size tag, the symbols and the legend keep working after you edit it. Changing a run's size or service after it is drawn is not built yet: erase it and draw it again, or move its pieces to another layer by hand (the size tag and schedule will still say the old service)."},{"slug":"mepdraft-symbols","scope":"addon:mepdraft","title":"MEP drafting: symbols, equipment and tags","summary":"Valves and accessories that cut into a run and heal it when they go, equipment in plan with connection points, and automatic tag numbers.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["valve","gate","globe","ball","butterfly","check","balancing","control valve","safety valve","pressure reducing","strainer","pump","flow meter","thermometer","gauge","expansion vessel","air vent","drain","union","flange","reducer","cap","damper","fire damper","attenuator","access door","radiator","fan coil","air handling unit","boiler","chiller","tank","heat exchanger","diffuser","grille","tag","attribute","renumber"],"headings":[{"id":"symbols-on-a-run","text":"Symbols on a run","level":2},{"id":"moving-and-erasing-symbols","text":"Moving and erasing symbols","level":2},{"id":"equipment","text":"Equipment","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"symbols-on-a-run\"\u003ESymbols on a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSYM\u003C/code\u003E, then the symbol&#039;s name (or \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E for the list), and click on a run. The symbol is turned to the run and the line is broken under it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a single-line run the symbol is as long as the scale says (6 mm on paper).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a double-line pipe it is sized from the pipe: three times the outside diameter long. On a duct it is as wide as the duct.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFlip\u003C/span\u003E turns the next symbols the other way, for the ones that show a direction (check valve, pump, reducer).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA click away from any run puts the symbol down free, for a schematic.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor pipes: gate, globe, ball, butterfly, check, balancing, control (with actuator), safety relief and pressure reducing valves; strainer, pump, flow meter; thermometer, pressure gauge, expansion vessel, air vent and drain (these stand on the line without breaking it); union, flanged joint, reducer and cap. For ducts: volume damper, fire damper, attenuator, flexible connection and access door.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe shapes are CadCraft&#039;s own drawings, in the general manner of building-services schematics (valves as two triangles tip to tip with the mark of the type at the middle, a pump as a circle with the flow triangle, a damper as a blade across the duct), as the symbol families of ISO 14617 and EN 12792 do. They were not checked against either standard, and a drawing made with them is not claimed to conform. Each symbol is a block (\u003Ccode\u003EMEP_SYM_BALL\u003C/code\u003E and so on): edit the block to make it yours.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-and-erasing-symbols\"\u003EMoving and erasing symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse the ordinary tools. Erase a valve and the run closes again. Move it along the run, or onto another run, and the old break is healed and a new one is cut where it lands; it turns to the run it lands on. Copy a valve onto a run and the copy cuts its own gap. Undo takes all of it back in one step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"equipment\"\u003EEquipment\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPEQUIP\u003C/code\u003E, then the kind: radiator, fan coil unit, air handling unit (a box with filter, coil and fan sections), boiler, chiller, tank or cylinder, heat exchanger, square four-way supply diffuser, linear diffuser, return or extract grille. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize\u003C/span\u003E takes millimetres, for example \u003Ccode\u003E1400\u003C/code\u003E for a radiator&#039;s length or \u003Ccode\u003E3000x1200\u003C/code\u003E for an air handling unit; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E turns the next ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe outlines are plain plan shapes at the size you type, not any maker&#039;s product. Each has connection points (short stubs for pipes, the middle of a side or the neck for ducts) that runs snap onto. Sanitary fixtures are not in this set: place those from the Library.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tags\"\u003ETags\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery tagged symbol and every equipment carries four attributes: \u003Ccode\u003ETAG\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETYPE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESIZE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESERVICE\u003C/code\u003E. The tag is shown, the others are kept for lists and the attribute export. Tags are numbered by kind as you place things: valves V-01, V-02; check valves NRV-01; control valves CV-01; pumps P-01; radiators RAD-01; air handling units AHU-01; fire dampers FD-01. Unions, flanges, reducers and caps carry no tag.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPTAG\u003C/code\u003E and a click changes one tag. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPTAG\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERenumber all\u003C/span\u003E numbers every tag again, top to bottom and left to right. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMEP drafting\u003C/span\u003E you can number by service and kind instead (HF-V-01).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Symbols on a run Type MEPSYM, then the symbol's name (or ? for the list), and click on a run. The symbol is turned to the run and the line is broken under it. On a single-line run the symbol is as long as the scale says (6 mm on paper). On a double-line pipe it is sized from the pipe: three times the outside diameter long. On a duct it is as wide as the duct. Flip turns the next symbols the other way, for the ones that show a direction (check valve, pump, reducer). A click away from any run puts the symbol down free, for a schematic. For pipes: gate, globe, ball, butterfly, check, balancing, control (with actuator), safety relief and pressure reducing valves; strainer, pump, flow meter; thermometer, pressure gauge, expansion vessel, air vent and drain (these stand on the line without breaking it); union, flanged joint, reducer and cap. For ducts: volume damper, fire damper, attenuator, flexible connection and access door. The shapes are CadCraft's own drawings, in the general manner of building-services schematics (valves as two triangles tip to tip with the mark of the type at the middle, a pump as a circle with the flow triangle, a damper as a blade across the duct), as the symbol families of ISO 14617 and EN 12792 do. They were not checked against either standard, and a drawing made with them is not claimed to conform. Each symbol is a block (MEP_SYM_BALL and so on): edit the block to make it yours. Moving and erasing symbols Use the ordinary tools. Erase a valve and the run closes again. Move it along the run, or onto another run, and the old break is healed and a new one is cut where it lands; it turns to the run it lands on. Copy a valve onto a run and the copy cuts its own gap. Undo takes all of it back in one step. Equipment MEPEQUIP, then the kind: radiator, fan coil unit, air handling unit (a box with filter, coil and fan sections), boiler, chiller, tank or cylinder, heat exchanger, square four-way supply diffuser, linear diffuser, return or extract grille. Size takes millimetres, for example 1400 for a radiator's length or 3000x1200 for an air handling unit; Angle turns the next ones. The outlines are plain plan shapes at the size you type, not any maker's product. Each has connection points (short stubs for pipes, the middle of a side or the neck for ducts) that runs snap onto. Sanitary fixtures are not in this set: place those from the Library. Tags Every tagged symbol and every equipment carries four attributes: TAG, TYPE, SIZE and SERVICE. The tag is shown, the others are kept for lists and the attribute export. Tags are numbered by kind as you place things: valves V-01, V-02; check valves NRV-01; control valves CV-01; pumps P-01; radiators RAD-01; air handling units AHU-01; fire dampers FD-01. Unions, flanges, reducers and caps carry no tag. MEPTAG and a click changes one tag. MEPTAG then Renumber all numbers every tag again, top to bottom and left to right. In Settings › CadCraft › MEP drafting you can number by service and kind instead (HF-V-01)."},{"slug":"mepdraft-lists","scope":"addon:mepdraft","title":"MEP drafting: legend, schedule and limits","summary":"The legend of what the drawing uses, the count schedule as a table and a CSV file, and what the add-on does not do yet.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["legend","schedule","count","quantity","table","csv","export","limits","not built","3d","bimcraft"],"headings":[{"id":"legend","text":"Legend","level":2},{"id":"schedule","text":"Schedule","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"legend\"\u003ELegend\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPLEGEND\u003C/code\u003E places a legend: a sample line of each service drawn in (on the service&#039;s own layer, so it shows its colour and linetype), then each symbol and each kind of equipment the drawing uses, with its name. It is one block, \u003Ccode\u003EMEP_LEGEND\u003C/code\u003E. Run the command again and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E after the drawing changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"schedule\"\u003ESchedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEPSCHED\u003C/code\u003E counts the symbols and equipment by kind, size and service, prints the count at the command line and places it as a table (block \u003Ccode\u003EMEP_SCHEDULE\u003C/code\u003E). It does not follow the drawing by itself: run the command again and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E writes the same count as a CSV file. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E and its attribute export work on the symbols too, since they are ordinary blocks with attributes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun lengths are not in the schedule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA run&#039;s size or service cannot be changed after it is drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipe sizes come from one table, steel pipe; other pipes are drawn at the outside diameter you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA branch opening in a double-line run is not closed again when the branch is erased.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESymbols do not go on flexible ducts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo sanitary fixtures, no fittings placed by themselves (tees and reducers at size changes), no slopes, no sizing or pressure-loss calculation. The Piping and HVAC add-ons do sizing and 3D solids for their own run objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing is read from or written to a BimCraft model: the 3D model and this 2D drawing are separate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe legend and schedule are placed in the space that is open (the model or a sheet) and list that space.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Legend MEPLEGEND places a legend: a sample line of each service drawn in (on the service's own layer, so it shows its colour and linetype), then each symbol and each kind of equipment the drawing uses, with its name. It is one block, MEP_LEGEND. Run the command again and choose Update after the drawing changed. Schedule MEPSCHED counts the symbols and equipment by kind, size and service, prints the count at the command line and places it as a table (block MEP_SCHEDULE). It does not follow the drawing by itself: run the command again and choose Update. EXPORT writes the same count as a CSV file. COUNT and its attribute export work on the symbols too, since they are ordinary blocks with attributes. Not built yet Run lengths are not in the schedule. A run's size or service cannot be changed after it is drawn. Pipe sizes come from one table, steel pipe; other pipes are drawn at the outside diameter you type. A branch opening in a double-line run is not closed again when the branch is erased. Symbols do not go on flexible ducts. No sanitary fixtures, no fittings placed by themselves (tees and reducers at size changes), no slopes, no sizing or pressure-loss calculation. The Piping and HVAC add-ons do sizing and 3D solids for their own run objects. Nothing is read from or written to a BimCraft model: the 3D model and this 2D drawing are separate. The legend and schedule are placed in the space that is open (the model or a sheet) and list that space."}]};
